   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the local finance law, in relation to the establishment of an\nelectronic open auction public bond sale pilot program solely for\nmunicipalities; and providing for the repeal of such provisions upon\nexpiration thereof",
    "basePrintNo": "S5886",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5886-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §58.10, Loc Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Local Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5886\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the local finance law, in relation to\nthe establishment of an electronic open auction public bond sale pilot\nprogram solely for municipalities; and providing for the repeal of such\nprovisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to establish a two year pilot program for\ncounties, cities, towns, villages, fire districts and school districts\nto sell municipal bonds via an open auction format instead of by a\nclosed bid format. Such open auctions would be conducted via an elec-\ntronic means instead of being done in person. This pilot program would\nbe used to assess the financial and administrative advantages of selling\nbonds via an open auction format by a nationally recognized electronic\nsecurities bidding platform.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Adds a new Local Finance Law section, 58.10 to establish a\nlocal electronic open auction public bond sale pilot program. The use of\nan open auction is entirely optional on the part of the local govern-\nment. The local government would also have the option of using the open\nauction public bond process for some of its bonds, while utilizing the\nstandard process for others. This section contains definitions for\npertinent terms, establishes the electronic open auction public bond\nsale pilot program and provides procedures for use of the open bid proc-\ness. For local governments utilizing this option, a report will be\nrequired at the end of the two year program.  The Comptroller is charged\nwith compiling the data received and subsequently reporting to the\nLegislature.\n\nSection two provides that this act shall take effect immediately, and\nthat it shall expire and be deemed repealed two years after such date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nNew York is one of only ten states that does not allow electronic\nbidding via an open auction format. Allowing local governments the\noption of utilizing this process can result in cost savings if the local\ngovernment is able to find lower interest rates. Open bond auctions have\nresulted in millions of dollars in savings for municipalities in other\nstates.\n\nGiven the financial stresses our local governments face, we should allow\nthem the flexibility to use the open bond process when it can result in\ncost savings for them.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the State.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately and shall sunset two years after it goes into effect.\n",
