    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §58.10, Loc Fin L",
    "lawSection": "Local Finance Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S5886A\n\nSPONSOR: GAUGHRAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the local finance law, in relation to\nestablishing an electronic open auction bond sale pilot program and\nproviding for the repeal of such provisions upon expiration thereof\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation would establish a two year electronic open auction bond\nsale pilot program to allow municipalities the option to sell municipal\nbonds in an open electronic auction format instead of by a closed elec-\ntronic bid format. This pilot program would be used to assess the finan-\ncial and administrative advantages of selling bonds via an open auction\nformat by a nationally recognized electronic securities bidding plat-\nform.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the local finance law by adding a new section 58.10.\n\n- Paragraph (a) adds a definition section\n\n- Paragraph (b) establishes an electronic open auction bond sale pilot\nprogram, authorizing municipalities to conduct open auction public bond\nsales operated through nationally recognized electronic securities\nbidding services, approved by the State Comptroller through an applica-\ntion process, for use by municipalities. Information regarding the\napproved nationally recognized electronic securities bidding services\nwill subsequently be posted on the website of the State Comptroller\n\n- Paragraph (c) provides that open auction electronic bidding will be\nconducted through certain nationally recognized electronic securities\nbidding services\n\n- Paragraph (d) states that, with certain exceptions, where the notice\nof sale has specified that bids will only be accepted electronically,\nthe municipality shall not be required to accept non-electronic bids\n\n-Paragraphs (e) and (f) provide that the program shall be administered\nby the municipality's chief fiscal officer, who will subsequently evalu-\nate its favorability over the method previously utilized by the munici-\npality\n\n-Paragraph (g) provides that the municipality's chief fiscal officer\nshall annually review the program to ensure its functionality and prof-\nitability, and provides certain procedures and requirements for the\nprogram's evaluation\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date of January 1st next succeeding the\ndate it has become law, expiring two years after such effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nElectronic bidding processes have been recognized to increase the speed\nand efficiency of bidding as well as reduce the administrative burden\nand costs of the paper bidding process. Currently, New York authorizes\nelectronic bidding through section 58 of the Local Finance Law, but only\npermits closed bidding. There are now forty states, including Califor-\nnia, New Jersey, Ohio and Texas that have enacted legislation to allow\nmunicipalities to conduct public bond sales through nationally recog-\nnized bidding firms, many of them by the electronic bidding open auction\nformat.\n\nOpen auctions are a cost saving tool that have allowed municipalities to\nfind lower interest rates and save money. When used with large bond\nofferings in other states, open auctions have translated into millions\nof dollars in savings for municipalities. With the focus on insuring\nfinancial efficiencies, throughout the state, municipalities must be\ngiven every possible option to save money.\n\nThis legislation would establish a two year pilot program for eligible\nmunicipalities, would have no financial impact on the state an d require\nnothing of the municipality. Simply, it allows for the use of another\ntool that has been developed through evolving technology, should munici-\npalities choose to utilize it.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:  None.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of January next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become a law and shall sunset 2 years after\nsuch date.\n",
